WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.620
- [Instructor] We're told
that Tomer has a bookshelf.

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:06.600
The table below shows how
many books are on each shelf.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.820
So the first shelf has 19 books,

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.210
the second shelf has 24 books,

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:13.440
the third shelf has 32 books,

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.670
and the fourth shelf has 7 books.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:20.950
How many books are on the
bookshelves all together?

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.430
So pause this video and see
if you can work that out

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:25.180
before we work through it together.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.960
All right, now let's
work through it together.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.850
So if we wanna figure out how many books

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:34.070
are on the bookshelves all together

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:37.380
or on the bookshelf altogether,
we essentially have to add

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.690
19 books to 24 books
to 32 books to 7 books.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:46.690
So really, this is going to
be 19 plus 24 plus 32 plus 7.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.410
That's what we really have to figure out.

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:52.810
Now, how can we do that?

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:55.540
Well, I like to do that by separating out

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:58.270
the tens place from the ones place.

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:03.270
For example, I could
rewrite 19 as 10 plus 9.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:09.130
I have one 10 and 9 ones, one 10, 9 ones.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:13.740
I could rewrite 24 as 20 plus 4.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.910
So plus 20 plus 4,

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:21.593
I could rewrite 32 as 30 plus 2.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:26.000
And last but not least, I could rewrite,

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.960
let me find a nice color
here, maybe I'll use blue,

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.160
I could rewrite 7.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:31.410
Well, actually, I want won't rewrite 7.

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:35.140
7 only has 7 ones, so I'll
just write 7 right there.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.580
And now what I can do is
I can take each of these

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:43.580
that came from our tens
places right over here.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:48.173
So I would have 10 plus 20,

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.090
I'm just crossing it out so
I can keep track of things,

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:55.250
plus 30, plus 30.

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:59.890
So I did these three, and then
I would have all of the ones

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:01.680
or the things that I
got from the ones place,

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.140
and let me do that in orange.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:06.800
So it's the 9, the 4, the 2, and the 7.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.800
So plus 9 plus 4 plus 2 plus 7.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:15.590
Now, what's 10 plus 20 plus 30?

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:20.590
10 plus 20 is 30, and then
that plus 30 is going to be 60.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:22.130
Another way to think about it,

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.560
I have one 10 plus another two tens,

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.500
which would give me three
tens, plus another three tens,

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.760
which would give me
six tens, so that's 60.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.230
And then how do I think about this?

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:34.730
Well, I can do this in any order.

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:38.900
I can see that 2 plus 7,
that's going to be equal to 9,

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:42.820
and I can see that 9
plus 9 is equal to 18.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:47.480
So this is going to be the
same thing as 18 plus 4.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:50.180
And then let's see it
takes two to get to 20,

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.370
and then I have another two.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:54.300
Actually, let me write it that way.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.870
So I could also write this as 60 plus,

00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:00.550
and if I just take 2 from
the 4 and put it onto the 18,

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:04.240
this would be 20 plus 2,

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.730
this and this are equivalent.

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:07.950
And the reason why I did that

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:11.000
is it's easy now to add 20 to 60.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.210
20 to 60, that's two tens plus six tens,

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:17.180
that's going to give us eight
tens, that's going to be 80.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:21.460
And then 80 plus 2 is, of
course, going to give us,

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:23.590
and we can have something of a drum roll,

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:25.960
almost have the answer, 82.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.363
That's how many books
Tomer has all together.

